  "articleBody": "Choosing the right baseboards and trim for a finished basement comes down to balancing moisture resistance against aesthetic preferences and budget. Because lower levels experience higher relative humidity and potential concrete wicking, material choice directly determines durability.\n\nPVC Trim\nPVC molding is completely waterproof and immune to rot, swelling, and mold. It performs best in utility areas, laundry rooms, or walkout basements where humidity fluctuates or minor dampness might occur. The tradeoff is that PVC expands and contracts more with seasonal temperature changes, requires specialized adhesives or trim screws, and has a slightly more flexible feel during installation.\n\nWood and Composite Trim\nPre-primed wood or composite trim offers classic profiles, sharp profiles, and great paintability. However, untreated wood or standard MDF readily absorbs subterranean moisture, leading to swollen edges, flaking paint, and mildew. If choosing wood or composite, your basement must have reliable humidity control and verified waterproofing, and all cut ends must be sealed before fastening.\n\nHow to Compare Your Options\nIf your space has any history of dampness, PVC delivers total peace of mind. If you prefer composite or wood, pair it with proper perimeter spacing above the concrete floor. HH Remodeling LLC provides meticulous trim carpentry throughout Denver and Aurora. Call Abraham Becerra at 303-505-0866 to plan your basement finish carpentry.",
